+# Agent Note: Think timing and activity-status placement
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-20-think-timing-and-tool-status-placement.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The [streamed Tool activity design](2026-08-20-streamed-tool-activity-feedback.md) made long calls visible, but Think had no equivalent elapsed time and the Tool timer/status arrangement did not read as one sentence. A completed Tool also needed an on-demand confirmation without shifting the summary, while a completed Think row did not need a `Done` label. Historical and interrupted reasoning had to remain truthful: the client could use existing event timestamps, but it could not invent a zero-duration timer for content whose streamed start was never loaded.
+
+## Decision
+
+`ui-conversation` derives a sparse timing lane beside the streamed Assistant block lane. The first `reasoning-delta` for an index records its existing Session event time. A `block-end`, the next non-reasoning chunk, the finalized Assistant message, or a step/turn interruption closes every open reasoning interval at that existing event boundary. Compaction moves blocks and their timings together, including providers that stream sparse block indexes. Finalized content that has no observed reasoning delta receives `null`, so replay renders no fabricated clock. These facts are client projection data only: no Session event, model message, Tool schema, execution order, or provider protocol changes.
+
+One shared elapsed-time primitive renders integer milliseconds below one second, whole seconds below one minute, and `m:ss` thereafter. While a reasoning block is active, its disclosure reads `Think ing <time> · <summary>` and retains the existing scanning glare. When it closes, the same row reads `Think <time> · <summary>` and freezes the clock. Hover and keyboard focus never add `Done` to Think.
+
+Every shipped Tool row receives the wrapper's perceptible activity state in addition to its durable success/error state. This keeps `ing` visible during the 600 ms fast-settlement presentation window even when the result has already arrived. A running Tool reads `<Tool> ing · <summary> <time>`; a timerless planned Code child reads `<Tool> ing`. On settlement, `ing` disappears while the timer stays after the summary. The completion seat reserves its width from the first settled render and reveals `Done` after the timer only on row hover or keyboard focus, preventing text movement. Custom keyed Tool views can consume the same additive presentation state; shipped generic, file, search, web, todo, question, Bash, and Skill rows do so.
+
+## Verification
+
+Conversation-node tests pin multi-block start/close boundaries, sparse-index compaction, finalized alignment, interruption freezing, and the absence of fabricated historical timing. Component tests pin live Think ticking, millisecond freezing, Think's permanent lack of `Done`, title-adjacent Tool `ing`, summary-adjacent timers, timerless planned children, fast-settlement perceptibility, the reserved completion seat, hover/focus `Done`, and reduced-motion exits. Client typechecking and the assembled Web build cover the public client contracts.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Time the whole Assistant step.** Rejected because one step may contain several reasoning blocks, text generation, and Tool-call generation. That duration would not describe the Think row beside which it appears.
+
+**Create a timer for finalized reasoning from the Assistant message timestamp.** Rejected because a completion timestamp alone cannot establish when reasoning began. Showing `0ms` would be precise-looking fiction.
+
+**Show `Done` on completed Think rows.** Rejected because frozen time and the absence of the running glare already communicate completion. The additional hover word adds noise without resolving ambiguity.
+
+**Keep Tool `ing` after the timer.** Rejected because activity state is scanned with the Tool name, while elapsed time qualifies the mutable summary. Separating those roles produces the intended `Code ing · summary 2s` reading.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Think and Tool activity now share timing typography and running motion while keeping different completion semantics. Live work is identifiable before a long operation returns, subsecond work remains meaningful, and hovering a completed Tool never reflows the transcript. Timing accuracy is bounded by existing Session event granularity; replay that lacks a streamed reasoning start intentionally omits the Think clock.
